{
  "requirement": "`OutlineContent::outline` exposes the outline of its whole retained file,\nincluding when the content value selects one item. The outline lists the file\nroot first, followed by its items in source preorder. Full selectors identify\nitems and their ancestry under {cite}`requirements.sdk.outlines.selector_syntax`.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl OutlineContent {\n    pub fn outline(&self) -> &Outline;\n}\n```\n\nThe root has an empty selector and covers the entire source snapshot.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The only structural obligation in `requirements.sdk.outlines.items` is the prescribed accessor: `impl OutlineContent { pub fn outline(&self) -> &Outline; }`. It must be a borrowing accessor on the retained content that exposes the whole-file outline, even when the value selects one item. The root `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/contents.rs#impl[OutlineContent]/fn[outline]` has that signature. It returns `&self.snapshot.outline`, the retained whole-file `Outline`. It does not use `selected` to narrow the result. `OutlineContent::select` clones the same `Snapshot` and only changes `selected`, so the whole-file result holds after selection. The root carries the marker for `requirements.sdk.outlines.items`.\n\nThe other clauses describe only observable behavior of the returned value: \"lists the file root first, followed by its items in source preorder\", \"The root has an empty selector and covers the entire source snapshot\", and the selector forms (cited from `requirements.sdk.outlines.selector_syntax`). A different implementation could build and order the list in any way, such as another parser or another traversal, and still produce the same outline. Those clauses therefore don't require any particular library, algorithm, type or delegation. The `Outline` type's fields are governed by `requirements.sdk.outlines.Outline`, and the selector syntax by the cited requirement. Neither is a structural obligation of this requirement. No further implementation roots are needed.\n\nbehavioral: Obligations from `requirements.sdk.outlines.items`: (1) `OutlineContent::outline` returns the outline of the whole retained file, even when the content selects one item. (2) The root comes first, followed by the items in source preorder. (3) The root has an empty selector and covers the entire source snapshot. Selector formats belong to `requirements.sdk.outlines.selector_syntax`, which is cited only, so I did not reassess them.\n\n**Whole-file outline for a selected item**\n- `selected_contents_outlive_handles_and_later_file_edits` selects the second line of a two-line file. It asserts `selected.outline().items.len() == 3`, so the outline holds the root plus both lines, not just the selected one.\n- `rust_items_attach_documentation_and_preserve_structural_hashes` selects `mod[auth]/fn[check]` and asserts `std::ptr::eq(function.outline(), contents.outline())`.\n- `markdown_heading_levels_resolve_encoded_duplicates_and_anonymous_items` does the same with `std::ptr::eq(second.outline(), contents.outline())`. It also calls `second.select(main)` on a selected item and gets the whole `# Main` section back.\n\n**Root first, empty selector, whole-source span**\n- `rust_items_attach_documentation_and_preserve_structural_hashes` asserts the first selector is \"\", the first kind is \"\", and `items[0].span == Span::new(0, source.len())`.\n- `markdown_heading_levels_resolve_encoded_duplicates_and_anonymous_items` asserts the root span covers the whole source.\n- `unicode_spans_use_bytes_and_whitespace_only_lines_create_no_items` asserts the root span is `0..source.len()` and the selector is \"\" for a multibyte source.\n- `unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors` asserts the root span is `0..18`, the empty-selector root for an empty file, and the root span `0..0` for empty text.\n\n**Source preorder**\n- The Rust test asserts the exact full selector sequence `\"\", mod[auth], mod[auth]/fn[check], mod[auth]/struct[Token], fn[last]`. That shows children follow their parent, and the next top-level sibling comes after the nested items.\n- The Markdown tests assert the ordered section lists, including nested sections, duplicates and a following peer section.\n- The plain-text tests assert items in source order (`alpha`, `beta`, `alpha`).\n- `bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline` covers the outline across all bundled languages.\n\nThese tests cover each obligation with concrete assertions, and I found no unmarked relevant tests or missing scenarios.",
  "references": "150caf1ba6bb4f96bea030bdec1ebe88e48408f4d4e211e53f466d8fb09f64b6",
